one of the major benefits of the ps5 and  xbox series consoles is hdmi 2.1  but what exactly are the benefits of  hdmi 2.1  and is it worth upgrading your tv for  the next-gen hardware  in short hdmi 2.1 is the latest version  of hdmi that distinguishes itself by  being capable of transmitting an  impressive bandwidth of 48 gigabits per  second in the cable  this is a huge improvement from the  previous 2.0 cables which carry 18  gigabits per second  bandwidth determines the max amount of  data the signal and the cable can send  the higher the resolution and the frame  rate the more data is needed  if you're playing a ps4 pro or xbox one  x in 4k  you've been using a 2.0 cable which can  support up to 4k  resolution at 60 frames a second with  static hdr  hdmi 2.1 is capable of 4k at 120  fps or 8k at 60fps  it also supports dynamic hdr lighting on  a frame by frame  basis as well as a color gamma of bt  2020  and 16 bit per color this means it's  capable of supporting tvs that have a  wide color gamut  making your image look even nicer hdmi  2.1 includes  auto low latency mode or allm  this will tell your tv that you're  playing a video game and instantly  switch it over to its low latency game  mode  setting hdmi 2.1 also has a vrr  a built-in variable refresh rate that  keeps your display's refresh rate in  tune with the supported device's frame  rate  so everything is smooth the new consoles  will support  vrr between 30 and 120 so what does this  all mean for your fancy new console  well both the ps5 and xbox series x use  hdmi 2.1 to enable games to run up to 4k  at 120 frames per second if you're  buying an xbox series s the console also  has an hdmi 2.1 port but it instead  targets a resolution of 1440p at 120fps  which hdmi 2.0 can already support  but using 2.1 on the series s will still  give you the benefits of dynamic hdr  as well as improved color and  subsampling the other benefit of hdmi  2.1  is 8k support both the ps5 and xbox  series x are capable of outputting 8k  but very few games will run in 8k for  the foreseeable future  meaning there isn't much of a reason to  worry about it for gaming  the big selling point of hdmi 2.1 right  now is the improved color  and 4k at 120 fps high frame rates like  120fps have typically been the domain of  pc gamers but now console gamers can  enjoy it too  a few examples of games that support 120  fps include devil may cry 5 special  edition and  dirt 5 as well as the multiplayer modes  in call of duty black ops cold war  and halo infinite a few previous gen  games will also be getting 120 fps  updates for the new consoles such as  gears 5  orion the will of the wisps and rainbow  six siege  as many pc gamers can attest frame rates  above 60 fps do lead to a smoother  gameplay experience and provide a  competitive edge in multiplayer  if you're interested in having games run  the best they can or want every  advantage and call of duty you can get  hdmi 2.1 is for you but what do you need  to actually get  hdmi 2.1 running for starters you'll  need a 2.1  cable presumably the ps5 and series x  will come with one  in the box but on the off chance they  don't and you need to buy one  look for the phrases ultra high speed  hdmi 2.1  and 48 gigabits per second always check  the specs on the product page  and look at reviews to make sure the  cable isn't cheaply made  the major thing you need to worry about  when it comes to your money though is  having a tv that supports hdmi 2.1  and 4k at 120 fps being a newer  technology you're only going to find  this in the latest models  and even then not all of them will  support it so make sure to check the  specs on the product page as well as  reviews  if you're planning on owning both  next-gen consoles or want to plug in  your pc as well  it's worth noting that most tvs with 2.1  only have it in  one hdmi port meaning you'll need to  swap around system cables  the only tv we're aware of at this  moment that has hdmi 2.1  in all of its ports is the new lg oled  cx series  it also supports g-sync and freesync  which is great for variable refresh  rates in pc gaming  and is in my personal opinion the best  high-end gaming tv on the market  that isn't ridiculously priced in fact  if this video does have you interested  in buying a tv we have an article over  on gamespot.com rounding up the best 4k  tvs for next gen  including the lg cx i just mentioned the  link is in the description below  but all of that said if hdmi 2.1 doesn't  sound like something you have to have  you can always wait until the technology  becomes more widespread and less  expensive  the reality is most games that aren't  multiplayer experiences  aren't going to support 120 fps for a  while and the ones that do might not  even be at 4k  resolution opting instead for 1440p or  even  1080p heck we'll probably still be  getting big budget graphical showcases  that shoot for 4k  30fps the improvements to color dynamic  hdr  and the variable refresh rate offered  with hdmi 2.1  are still major advancements that more  next-gen games will take advantage of  but how worthwhile these improvements  are is all dependent on how you feel  about your current tv  if you just upgraded your tv recently  and don't care about 120 fps  i wouldn't worry about hdmi 2.1 but if  you've been waiting to get a whole new  setup for the next gen then definitely  consider hdmi 2.1 when looking at new  tvs  if for nothing else then the give  yourself more options and how you play  your games  [Music]  you
 

